WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:02.980
This story is sponsored by MarketerHire.

00:00:02.980 --> 00:00:06.340
In January, something strange happened in
North Korea.

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:09.200
The entire nation’s internet kept blacking
out.

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:13.580
The notoriously restrictive country runs a
few dozen websites from the booking site for

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:19.080
its national airline to a page that serves
as the official portal for Kim Jong-un's government.

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:21.620
The timing of the outages was peculiar.

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:25.690
They coincided with a bunch of North Korean
missile tests…prompting suspicions that

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:28.710
the US Defense Department might have been
behind it.

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:33.629
After all, a single independent hacker couldn’t
have pulled off such an audacious attack.

00:00:33.629 --> 00:00:34.670
Could they?

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:40.260
Well, Wired magazine interviewed an American
hacker who does claim to be behind the blackouts.

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:44.400
According to the news outlet, they’ve seen
screen recordings to back up his claims.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:48.079
He’s known only as “P4x” due to concerns
for his safety.

00:00:48.079 --> 00:00:51.130
He says he carried out his attacks for revenge.

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:56.969
In January 2021, North Korean hackers attacked
him and other security researchers who used

00:00:56.969 --> 00:00:59.199
Windows PCs and Google Chrome.

00:00:59.199 --> 00:01:03.420
Google says the North Korean hackers set up
Twitter accounts pretending to be cybersecurity

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:08.360
bloggers and shared links with their victims
to a blog with malware that contained a backdoor

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:11.830
to try to access the victims’ device remotely
without their knowledge.

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:14.580
Google never said how successful the hackers
were.

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:19.690
P4x was shocked that North Korea tried to
target him though in his case, they didn’t

00:01:19.690 --> 00:01:20.690
succeed.

00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:24.630
Nonetheless, it left him “deeply unnerved”
by the attack and also what he felt was a

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:27.010
lack of response from the U.S. government.

00:01:27.010 --> 00:01:32.320
So, he slowly cooked up a plan to get even,
waiting a year to launch an attack of his

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:33.320
own.

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:37.780
He says he took North Korea offline while
in his pajamas watching the film Aliens and

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:40.240
munching on spicy corn snacks!

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:44.360
North Korea News, an information source that
provides news about the country, reported

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:49.640
a Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS) attack
on January 14 followed by another on January

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:50.640
26.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:54.500
P4x says he targeted “vulnerabilities”
in North Korean systems.

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:57.960
He didn’t describe what those are because
he doesn’t want Pyongyang to defend against

00:01:57.960 --> 00:01:59.430
them in the future.

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:04.320
He did give an example of a bug that mishandles
certain HTTP headers that allowed him to flood

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:07.780
the servers, overwhelming them and knocking
them offline.

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:10.160
Almost every North Korean website went down.

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:12.260
So did email and all internet-based services.

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:16.730
Though it didn’t affect access to sites
hosted outside that country.

00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:18.890
But what did the hack achieve?

00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:23.140
After all, virtually no citizens in the hermit
kingdom can access the internet.

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:28.290
Only a few thousand have special permission
to go online in a country of nearly 26 million.

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:32.570
So as hard as it is for foreigners to understand
what’s happening inside North Korea, it’s

00:02:32.570 --> 00:02:36.770
even harder for North Koreans to understand
anything about the outside world.

00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:39.910
P4x admits his own hacking was symbolic.

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:43.130
Akin to tearing down government banners or
defacing buildings.

00:02:43.130 --> 00:02:47.210
He told Wired he did it to send a message
to North Korea that he wouldn’t tolerate

00:02:47.210 --> 00:02:52.069
their hacking: “If they don’t see we have
teeth, it’s just going to keep coming.”

00:02:52.069 --> 00:02:55.540
North Korea’s cyber warfare has caught the
West off guard.

00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:59.870
Ironically, a country that’s closed itself
off from the rest of the world has produced

00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:02.520
some of the world’s most efficient hackers.

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:07.130
7,000 North Koreans are estimated to be employed
in their country’s cyber program.

00:03:07.130 --> 00:03:11.260
They often operate abroad, in countries like
China where there’s better access to information

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:13.230
than in their isolated homeland.

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:18.260
These three men wanted by the FBI are suspected
of belonging to a unit of North Korean hackers

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:22.590
dubbed the Lazarus Group - a reference to
the biblical figure raised from the dead by

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:23.620
Jesus.

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:28.450
The Lazarus Group is believed to be behind
some of the costliest cyberattacks in history.

00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:32.881
Like the infamous Sony leak - where hackers
released five Sony Pictures films and a host

00:03:32.881 --> 00:03:35.090
of confidential data in 2014.

00:03:35.090 --> 00:03:39.250
They threatened more action if Sony dared
to release the film “The Interview”, a

00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:44.430
comedy featuring Seth Rogan and James Franco
about two Americans who assassinate Kim Jong

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:45.430
Un.

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:50.739
In 2016, the Lazarus Group nearly stole $1
billion from Bangladesh’s national bank.

00:03:50.739 --> 00:03:53.690
The ONLY reason they didn’t succeed is because
of a fluke.

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:57.840
When they tried to transfer the money to a
bank located on “Jupiter Street” - the

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:01.660
word “Jupiter” triggered alarms because
it’s the name of a sanctioned Iranian shipping

00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:02.660
vessel.

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:07.379
Lazarus Group has crippled the computer systems
of Boeing, the UK’s National Health Service,

00:04:07.379 --> 00:04:12.239
and Germany’s federal railway - demanding
payment in Bitcoin to unfreeze their systems.

00:04:12.239 --> 00:04:14.490
North Korean hackers seem to love crypto.

00:04:14.490 --> 00:04:20.840
They stole nearly $400 million in cryptocurrencies
in 2021, according to the data platform Chainalysis.

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:25.590
The United Nations believes much of that money
is used to fund North Korea’s weapons program,

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:27.810
including developing nuclear missiles.

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:33.250
So how does a country where most people can’t
even access a computer produce elite cyber-criminals?

00:04:33.250 --> 00:04:37.530
The North Korean hackers are cultivated in
the same way Olympians in China are trained

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:39.510
and go on to dominate the Games.

00:04:39.510 --> 00:04:41.150
They’re recruited when they’re young.

00:04:41.150 --> 00:04:45.410
Promising students who excel at math are placed
in specialized schools and trained in advanced

00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:48.000
coding, with further training at specialized
universities.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:52.270
And they show off their coding prowess at
international coding competitions.

00:04:52.270 --> 00:04:54.060
The New Yorker put it this way:

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:58.430
The coding and the analytical skills on display
at such events were like the Force in the

00:04:58.430 --> 00:05:03.770
“Star Wars” movies: it could be used for
the light side, or for the dark.

00:05:03.770 --> 00:05:08.620
As for P4X, temporarily shutting down North
Korea’s internet was simply the appetizer

00:05:08.620 --> 00:05:10.190
before the main course.

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:14.280
He says he intends to go further by trying
to hack into North Korea’s systems to steal

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:17.270
information and share it with experts.

00:05:17.270 --> 00:05:21.110
According to Wired, he’s hoping to recruit
more people to his cause and apparently launched

00:05:21.110 --> 00:05:24.780
a site on the dark web called “FUNK Project”.

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:27.940
Can you take a stab at what that might stand
for?

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:30.520
“FU North Korea”.

